   5. cite73†G.5 Martingale Root Bound for Nested WAIT L84:   17. cite74†H Additional Experiments L85:     1. cite75†H.1 Nested WAIT implementation pseudocode L86:     2. cite76†H.2 Threshold without waiting L87:     3. cite77†H.3 Real-GPU validation L88:       1. cite78†Simulator vs GPU. L89:       2. cite79†Single-type workload on GPU. L90:       3. cite80†Real dataset on GPU. L91:     4. cite81†H.4 Repeated-run variability L92:     5. cite82†H.5 Prefill-decode-disaggregated deployment L93: cite83†License: CC BY-NC-SA 4.0†info.arxiv.org L94: \nL95: arXiv:2504.11320v4 [cs.LG] 13 Jun 2026\nL96: # Optimizing LLM Inference: Fluid-Guided Online Scheduling with Memory Constraints\nL97: Ruicheng Ao ^{†}^{†}thanks: E-mail: aorc@mit.edu, luogan@stu.pku.edu.cn, dslevi@mit.edu, xinshang.w@alibaba-inc.com. Affiliation: Institute for Data, Systems, and Society, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, MA 02139    Gan Luo Affiliation: School of Mathematical Sciences, Peking University, Beijing 100871    David Simchi-Levi Affiliation: Institute for Data, Systems, and Society, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, MA 02139    Xinshang Wang Affiliation: Alibaba Group\nL98: August 24, 2026\nL99: ###### Abstract\nL100: Large language models now serve millions of users daily, with providers incurring costs exceeding $700,000 per day. Each request requires token-by-token inference, making GPU scheduling central to latency, capacity, and cost. The difficulty is endogenous memory growth: generated tokens expand the Key-Value (KV) cache, and overflow can evict in-progress requests and waste prior computation.\nL101: We formulate inference as a multi-stage online scheduling problem with endogenous memory growth, linear iteration times, and GPU-resident KV-cache constraints. We introduce a fluid model that characterizes equilibrium batch composition, memory requirement, and the fluid stability region.\nL102: Guided by the fluid model, we design WAIT (Waiting for Accumulated Inference Threshold), a threshold-based admission rule for known output lengths, and Nested WAIT, which extends the rule to unknown output lengths by regulating how requests advance across decode-stage segments.\nL103: Under the stated threshold and memory conditions, WAIT approximates the fluid benchmark asymptotically; for unknown output lengths, Nested WAIT obtains guarantees on throughput, latency, and eviction avoidance under an additional safety buffer.\nL104: In Vidur simulations configured for Llama-2-7B on an A100 GPU, with supplemental real-GPU validation reported in the appendix, the policies enlarge the empirically observed stable operating range relative to the tested baseline configurations and reduce latency especially in near-overloaded and overloaded regimes.\nL105: Keywords: Large Language Model, Key-value cache, Memory Constraint, Online scheduling\nL106: ## 1 Introduction\nL107: Large Language Models (LLMs) now dominate natural language processing (NLP) (cite84†Devlin et al., 2019 ; cite85†Brown et al., 2020 ; cite86†Kaplan et al., 2020 ; cite87†Ouyang et al., 2022 ; cite88†Wei et al., 2022a ; cite89†Wei et al., 2022b ; cite90†Touvron et al., 2023 ; cite91†OpenAI, 2024 ), powering chatbots (cite92†Anthropic, 2023 ; cite93†Bai et al., 2023 ; cite91†OpenAI, 2024 ; cite94†DeepSeek-AI, 2025 ), search engines (cite95†Google, 2023 ; cite96†Microsoft, 2023 ), and programming assistants (cite97†GitHub, 2022 ; cite98†Anthropic, 2025 ).\nL108: These models generate text through inference, which imposes significant computational demands on memory and processing resources (cite99†García-Martín et al., 2019 ). Systems like ChatGPT incur daily inference costs exceeding $700,000 and contribute to substantial carbon emissions (cite100†Patel, 2023 ; cite101†Patterson et al., 2021 ).\nL109: Efficient inference scheduling can reduce these operational costs and energy consumption (cite102†Strubell et al., 2019 ; cite103†Desislavov et al., 2021 ) by balancing system throughput and response latency (cite104†Wu et al., 2022 ).\nL110: Figure cite105†1 illustrates how a query is processed during LLM inference. The user submits a prompt (e.g., “Is apple a fruit?”), which then passes through two computational phases:\nL111: \nL112:   * •\nL113: Prefill phase (stage 0). Upon receiving the prompt, the model first tokenizes the input into a sequence of discrete units (e.g., “Is”, “apple”, “a”, “fruit”, “?”). These tokens are then embedded and simultaneously processed in a single forward pass to compute the Key-Value (KV) cache, which stores intermediate representations (i.e., attention keys and values) for each token. These precomputed values enable efficient reuse during subsequent decoding steps.\nL114: This phase corresponds to Stage 0 in Figure cite105†1 , where all prompt tokens are embedded and their KV representations are added to the cache.\nL115:   * •\nL116: Decode phase (stages 1 to $l^{\\prime}$). After the prefill phase, the model enters the decode phase, where it generates the output one token at a time. At each decode stage, the model queries the existing KV cache to compute the next token, appends the new token to the output sequence, and updates the KV cache with its key-value pair. For instance, the model might first generate “Yes” (Stage 1), then “it” (Stage 2), followed by “is” (Stage 3), and finally the “End of Sequence” (EOS) token (Stage 4).\nL117: This progression is depicted in the lower part of Figure cite105†1 . Each token generation step involves both reading from and writing to the KV cache, so the KV cache grows linearly with the length of the generated sequence.\nL118: Figure cite105†1 also highlights key performance metrics in LLM inference:\nL119: \nL120:   * •\nL121: \nL122: Time-to-first-token (TTFT) measures the latency from user input to the first generated token.\nL123: \nL124:   * •\nL125: \nL126: Latency refers to the total time required to complete the generation of all output tokens.\nL127: \nL128:   * •\nL129: \nL130: Throughput captures the average number of tokens generated per unit time.\nL131: This inference structure, particularly the growing memory requirements and sequential decoding pattern, introduces fundamental constraints on scheduling and batching. Efficient scheduling must account for both prompt heterogeneity and KV cache dynamics to balance latency and resource use.\nL132: \nL133: cite106†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: An example of LLM inference.\nL134: Scheduling LLM inference tasks involves grouping prompts into batches processed concurrently on a GPU. Batching improves throughput: processing multiple prompts together amortizes the fixed overhead of each iteration and better uses GPU parallelism. However, this benefit comes with a fundamental tension: larger batches consume more memory through their combined KV caches.\nL135: The KV cache improves efficiency, as it prevents the model from recalculating attention history for each new token; without it, computational cost would scale quadratically with sequence length. But the KV cache also grows dynamically during decode, making memory consumption unpredictable. This creates a core trade-off: larger batches increase throughput but consume more memory, risking capacity overflow.\nL136: Memory overflow can occur in several practical scenarios: (i) bursty arrivals, where sudden traffic spikes temporarily exceed capacity; (ii) unknown output lengths, where prompts generate longer responses than anticipated; and (iii) multi-tenant environments, where multiple users compete for GPU memory.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2504.11320] Optimizing LLM Inference: Fluid-Guided Online Scheduling with Memory Constraints (https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.11320)\nciteturn53view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; 